+# Shepherd shadow replay and non-circular control-plane break-fix
+
+Issue: `2c4dff71-4cb0-4530-be49-ebb2cb281039`  
+Type: P0 standard/control-plane reliability  
+Planning base: `964e3daf94f20391062e3cf642c34b4bd193cda4`
+
+## Purpose
+
+Make Shepherd control-plane changes observable before they become authoritative. The installed trusted runtime and a candidate runtime must evaluate the same retained, privacy-bounded operand bundle. A disagreement that survives one fresh reconciliation, or remains for 15 minutes, becomes a durable control-plane incident. Candidate code is shadow-only: it cannot project the canonical verdict, select or execute an auto-action, merge, close an issue, or activate itself.
+
+When the incident points to a control-plane defect, the operator gets a short, stable-base break-fix lane: freeze the affected product lane, branch the hotfix from an explicitly pinned trusted base, review it independently, merge it with the existing exact-head gates, verify the installed artifact against retained bundles, explicitly activate that verified artifact, then resume the product lane.
+
+## Existing seams and constraints
+
+- `lib/pr-pull.js` owns the canonical normalized PR read and `computeVerdict`; the replay contract must capture the exact operands passed to that pure computation rather than invent a second verdict model.
+- `lib/pr-monitor/gather.js` derives monitor snapshots from the same read, and `lib/pr-monitor/journal.js` already provides append-only, git-common-dir-scoped event storage and serialized journal writes.
+- `lib/pr-monitor/auto-actions.js` separately decides bounded Tier-A actions from canonical evidence. Shadow output must never enter this module.
+- `lib/pr-monitor/reconcile-executor.js` owns daemon convergence and the live Shepherd lease. It is the only appropriate integration point for a post-reconciliation comparison.
+- `lib/kernel/sqlite-driver.js` gives the canonical `kernel_pr` row freshest-head precedence. This increment must not add a second verdict authority or widen the Kernel schema unless a later task proves the journal cannot satisfy retention.
+- `lib/commands/merge.js` independently rechecks exact head, issue lease, live protected checks, threads, PR/issue binding, and configured rules. Shadow or incident state is evidence only and cannot bypass or replace those checks.
+- Existing safety stays intact: Shepherd never merges or resolves review threads; Tier-A remains bounded and required-check-only; unreadable or torn evidence fails closed.
+- Planning and implementation stay isolated from dirty/untracked files in the primary checkout.
+
+## Selected design
+
+### 1. Versioned replay envelope
+
+Capture a deterministic `forge.shepherd-replay.v1` envelope immediately around the existing `computeVerdict` call. It contains:
+
+- subject: repository, PR number, exact base ref/SHA when available, exact head SHA, and capture time;
+- evaluator inputs: only the normalized, bounded operands consumed by `computeVerdict`, including required-check policy, observations, threads/reviews, conflict/divergence, lifecycle and settle-clock inputs;
+- provenance: controller runtime identity, Forge package version, source commit if resolvable, schema version, and a SHA-256 content hash over canonical JSON;
+- completeness: explicit unreadable/torn surfaces and cap/truncation markers.
+
+The envelope excludes tokens, environment values, raw CI logs, arbitrary comment bodies, and filesystem paths. IDs/names already necessary for verdict reasoning remain bounded and fenced as untrusted data. Unknown fields, invalid timestamps, excessive cardinality, missing exact head, hash mismatch, or unsupported schema make replay `INCOMPLETE`; they never produce a clean/authoritative result. At least one valid bundle per active incident is retained atomically under the existing git-common-dir Shepherd journal root. Retention is bounded by count and bytes.
+
+### 2. Pure dual-runtime replay
+
+The trusted controller evaluates the envelope locally with the installed evaluator and invokes the candidate through a constrained child-process adapter. The child receives only the immutable envelope on stdin and may return only a bounded schema `{schemaVersion, runtime, bundleHash, verdict, evidenceHash}` on stdout. It gets no GitHub token, Kernel handle, journal write path, merge arguments, or action history.
+
+The trusted controller validates runtime identity, exact bundle hash, enum membership, output size, timeout, and exit status. It computes the comparison itself. Candidate timeout, crash, malformed output, mismatched hash, or unsupported schema is `INCOMPLETE`, not agreement. Agreement is exact canonical-verdict equality plus the stable hash of verdict-relevant evidence; presentation text is ignored.
+
+### 3. Persistent contradiction incidents
+
+Comparison state is a pure transition machine keyed by `repo + PR + head + bundleHash + installedRuntime + candidateRuntime`.
+
+- First contradiction: append `shadow.contradiction_observed`, retain the bundle, and request one normal fresh reconciliation. No incident yet.
+- Same contradiction on the next complete reconciliation: append one idempotent `control_plane.incident_opened` immediately.
+- If no complete reconciliation arrives, a trusted-controller tick at or after first-observed + 15 minutes opens the same incident.
+- Agreement before either threshold appends `shadow.contradiction_cleared` and resets that key.
+- Head/runtime/schema changes end the old observation as stale and start a new key; they never carry elapsed time across subjects.
+- Incomplete evidence neither clears nor confirms a contradiction. It retains the first-observed time, emits bounded diagnostics, and remains fail-closed.
+
+The incident record contains reproduction command, bundle path/hash, exact runtimes, installed/candidate verdicts, timestamps, subject SHA, and state (`open`, `verified`, `activated`, `closed`). It contains no raw secret or log body. Append and transition occur under the existing per-PR journal lock so duplicate daemon ticks are idempotent.
+
+Canonical operation continues from the installed verdict unless an incident policy explicitly freezes the affected product lane. Candidate output is never passed to kernel verdict projection or `decideBoundedAutoActions`.
+
+### 4. Stable-base break-fix and activation
+
+An open incident yields an operator-oriented, machine-readable break-fix receipt and instructions:
+
+1. Freeze only the affected repo/PR/head product lane; other disjoint lanes continue.
+2. Select and record `trustedBaseSha` from the currently installed, last-known-good controller. Create a dedicated hotfix worktree from that exact SHA with a separate Forge issue/lease.
+3. Scope the hotfix to the incident's control-plane files and retained replay test. The product branch is never the hotfix base and cannot approve/review itself.
+4. Ship through an independent reviewer and existing protected CI/exact-head merge path. The candidate runtime remains shadow-only throughout review.
+5. From the trusted controller, verify the merged artifact/package identity against the retained bundle corpus. Verification must show deterministic replay and no contradictions; failure or unreadable provenance blocks activation.
+6. A human invokes activation from the trusted controller and supplies the verified artifact identity plus receipt. Activation rechecks that controller identity differs from the candidate under test, that the artifact is merged/reachable from the declared stable line, that verification matches the exact artifact and bundle hashes, and that the incident is still current.
+7. After activation, a fresh installed-runtime reconciliation must agree. Only then mark the incident closed and resume the frozen product lane.
+
+The activation surface records a receipt; it does not merge the hotfix, mutate GitHub review threads, or weaken branch protection. Candidate processes cannot create a valid receipt because only the trusted controller owns the journal lock and validates the expected controller/artifact identities. If provenance cannot distinguish trusted controller from candidate, activation refuses.
+
+## Operator experience
+
+The normal happy path remains quiet: no extra comments, labels, or prompts when installed and candidate agree. `forge shepherd <pr> --pull --json` remains compatible.
+
+On first disagreement, one concise local event shows the two runtime identities, exact head, bundle hash, and that a reconciliation is pending. On incident, output leads with the freeze scope and a copyable replay command, then the stable-base hotfix/verify/activate sequence. The operator does not need to reconstruct GitHub queries or compare logs manually. JSON output is authoritative; human text is a projection.
+
+## Performance and boundedness
+
+- Capture reuses the already-read canonical operands; it performs no second GitHub read.
+- Installed replay is pure and in-process. Candidate replay is one bounded subprocess per newly observed bundle/runtime pair, cached by content key.
+- Default limits: 1 MiB envelope, 64 KiB candidate output, 10-second candidate timeout, 64 retained bundles or 64 MiB per PR (whichever comes first). Final constants may be reduced by focused benchmarks, never raised without a test.
+- The daemon does not block action convergence on a shadow timeout; it records `INCOMPLETE` and canonical installed behavior proceeds unless an open incident freezes that exact lane.
+- Journal reads for transition state are tail/index bounded; implementation must not rescan an unbounded NDJSON history on every tick.
+
+## Security and privacy review
+
+- Authorization (OWASP A01): trusted installed controller retains all write/action/activation authority; candidate has none.
+- Insecure design (A04): exact subject/runtime/hash keys, one-reconciliation/15-minute thresholds, and fail-closed provenance prevent self-approval and stale carryover.
+- Integrity (A08): canonical serialization and content hashes bind operands, outputs, verification, and activation receipts.
+- Logging (A09): durable, bounded incident events include reproduction provenance without secrets or raw log/comment bodies.
+- Untrusted candidate output is size-capped, parsed as data, enum-validated, never interpolated into a shell, and fenced in human output.
+
+## Rejected approaches
+
+- Letting the candidate write the canonical Kernel verdict or trigger reruns: circular authority and unsafe.
+- Comparing current GitHub reads made separately by each runtime: network/head drift would create false contradictions and non-reproducible evidence.
+- Replaying the presentation bundle or rendered summary: those are lossy projections, not the canonical verdict operands.
+- Opening an incident on the first mismatch: too noisy for transient runtime/load differences; the specified reconciliation/15-minute barrier preserves urgency without alert churn.
+- Adding a second database/table immediately: the existing append-only journal, lock, and bounded local retention are sufficient for MVP; schema growth can follow measured need.
+- Auto-merging or auto-activating the hotfix: violates the independent human/protected-gate checkpoint.
+
+## Delivery topology
+
+Two stacks share only the contract PR, then proceed without overlapping file ownership:
+
+```text
+S0 replay contract
+├── S1 shadow comparator + incident state machine
+│   └── S2 Shepherd capture/daemon/operator integration
+└── H0 stable-base break-fix verification + activation receipt
+```
+
+- **S0** is small and foundational; merge first.
+- **S1** and **H0** may be reviewed and merged in parallel after S0 because they own disjoint files.
+- **S2** stacks only on S1 and wires the existing Shepherd seams. It does not own H0 files.
+- An incident-specific production hotfix is a separate branch from the pinned trusted base, not another commit in S0/S1/S2/H0.
+
+## Acceptance and release gates
+
+1. A retained envelope replays byte-for-byte deterministically and produces the same canonical verdict/evidence hash across installed invocations.
+2. Candidate agreement is quiet and never changes canonical verdict/action output.
+3. A mismatch opens exactly one incident after the next complete reconciliation or at 15 minutes, whichever happens first.
+4. Malformed, oversized, stale-head, timeout, hash-mismatch, null/invalid clock, unreadable policy, and unknown-schema cases fail closed without action.
+5. Candidate code cannot access action/merge/Kernel/activation dependencies and cannot create or validate its own activation receipt.
+6. Hotfix verification binds exact stable base, merged artifact, bundle corpus, controller identity, and activation receipt; product-lane resume requires fresh installed-runtime agreement.
+7. Existing Shepherd, PR-monitor, merge, Kernel PR-write, strict lint, generated-manifest, Windows paths/line endings, and package-smoke suites remain green.
+8. No production implementation begins until the human accepts the trust boundary, thresholds, retention limits, and stacked delivery topology below.
+
+## Human design checkpoint
+
+Approve or amend these four decisions before `/dev`:
+
+1. Candidate is subprocess-isolated, read-only, and never authoritative.
+2. Incident threshold is **one subsequent complete reconciliation OR 15 minutes**, whichever comes first.
+3. MVP persistence uses the existing bounded per-PR journal rather than a new Kernel table.
+4. Activation remains an explicit human checkpoint executed by a distinct trusted controller against a merged, verified artifact.
+
